WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.760
Pop quiz: How many megabytes do you think
it takes to stream an episode of Cobra Kai

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.760
to your phone?

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:08.120
How about uploading a TikTok?

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.120
Listening to a podcast?

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.320
If you’re like most people, you have no
idea.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.929
And yet, when you go to buy a cell phone,
the sales rep will present a bunch of different

00:00:16.929 --> 00:00:21.369
data plans to you, as if you know how many
gigs you're going to use over the next 30

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:22.369
days.

00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:26.600
That’s why most people end up going with
what seems like the safest option: unlimited

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:27.949
data.

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:28.949
Sounds good, right?

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:31.919
I mean, I use my phone a lot, and I don’t
want to count megabytes.

00:00:31.919 --> 00:00:36.680
Sure, it’s more expensive than the capped
plans, but at least I’ll know that I won’t

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:40.250
run out of data in the middle of a road trip
or an important video conference.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:45.746
Before you make that call, there’s a few
things you should know about “unlimited” plans.

00:00:45.746 --> 00:00:46.772
It's data.

00:00:46.772 --> 00:00:48.257
Data.

00:00:56.974 --> 00:01:00.490
First, unlimited plans are not exactly unlimited.

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:06.289
They usually still have a data cap and once
you go over that, your speeds will slow down,

00:01:06.289 --> 00:01:08.659
depending on how busy the network is.

00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:14.219
Secondly, many carriers will sometimes give
capped accounts higher speeds than unlimited

00:01:14.219 --> 00:01:15.219
ones.

00:01:15.219 --> 00:01:19.380
The reason is that when capped customers go
over their limit, they can be charged extra,

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.909
so there’s incentive for the carrier to
let them rip through their data as quickly

00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:24.450
as possible.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.780
Unlimited customers have already paid everything
they’re gonna pay, so why make it easy for

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.000
them to use data?

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.950
It’s kind of like an all-you-can-eat buffet
that deliberately uses small plates to keep

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.780
the diners from eating too fast.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.619
Third, and this is the big one: you probably
don’t need that much data anyway.

00:01:42.619 --> 00:01:49.020
According to a 2018 study, the average usage
by unlimited data customers was only 6.1 GB

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.090
per month.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:55.149
That may seem strangely low, but it’s because
you only use your mobile data when you’re

00:01:55.149 --> 00:01:58.509
not connected to a WiFi network... and WiFi
is everywhere.

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:02.450
At home, at work, on campus, at the gym and
the coffee shop.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:07.520
Sure, there’s a small number of people who
might rely on mobile data, like if you do

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.690
a lot of business on the road, or don’t
have broadband at home and use your phone

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:13.010
to stream movies.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:18.930
But for most of us, “mobile data” represents
a small percentage of our overall usage.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:22.400
So then why are most of us subscribed to unlimited
plans?

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.710
Mainly, because that’s what the service
providers want us to do.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:31.610
Unlimited plans are heavily promoted by the
big carriers, often bundled with phone discounts

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.790
and free subscriptions to streaming platforms.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:39.530
In fact, you have to dig pretty deep into
their websites to find any alternatives.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.510
And most of the big wireless companies have
started making their capped data plans “pre-paid”

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:49.070
only, which means you have to pay for a whole
year of service up front.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:51.510
They really want you to choose unlimited.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.250
And why wouldn’t they?

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:58.560
With the cheapest “Premium Network Access”
plan offering 50GB a month, that means the

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.890
average customer is using less than a quarter
of the data they’re paying for!

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:07.460
That would be like paying for 100 gallons
of gas every month, even though you only use

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:08.460
20.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:12.290
And you don’t get credit for the gas you
didn’t use--it goes right back to the oil

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.290
company.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:19.450
Now, if you happen to be one of these people
who actually uses 50 to 100 gigabytes of mobile

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:24.660
data a month--you might want to cut down on
your screen time--but, you are getting a really

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:25.660
good deal.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.840
That’s way less per megabyte than the average
rate, but only because all those other customers

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.930
are paying for megabytes they’re not using--essentially
subsidizing your low price.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:39.900
This is similar to the business model of many
gyms and fitness clubs.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.360
Most of their members barely use the services
they pay for, which keeps the price low for

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.400
those who work out regularly.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.960
So how much money can you actually save by
forgoing unlimited data plans?

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:52.730
I think it’s time to…

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:54.167
RUN THE NUMBERS!

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:03.500
One year ago, Kevin got a new phone from one
of the major wireless carriers, and the sales

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.460
rep strongly encouraged him to go with an
unlimited plan.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:11.440
Kevin travels a lot for work, and relies on
his phone when on the road, so he figures

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.100
he’s probably a slightly above average data
user.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:20.950
He opts for the mid-tier option: 50GB of data
without slow-down for $75 dollars a month

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:22.400
(plus fees and taxes).

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.570
A year later, Kevin happens to check his data
usage and finds that he used on average about

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:32.360
7.5 GB a month--only 15% of the total he’s
paying for!

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:37.070
At first, he thinks that can’t be right,
but then he starts remembering all the places

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:43.510
he typically uses his phone: airports, cafes,
hotels, convention centers--all with WiFi.

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:48.230
Not wanting to spend money on something he
doesn’t use, he digs through the carrier’s

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:54.780
website and finds that they have an 8GB pre-paid
plan for only $25 a month--if you pay for

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.300
the whole year up front.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:02.320
That’s $300 now versus $900 over the course
of the year--an easy decision for Kevin, who

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.620
happens to have enough cash on hand.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:09.870
There is one drawback to Kevin’s plan: at
only 8 GB a month, he will have to be a bit

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.390
more careful about how he uses his data.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:16.880
Fortunately, there are some simple options
in your phone’s settings to minimize usage

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:22.480
outside of WiFi, like turning off push notifications,
automatic app updates and GPS when you’re

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:23.480
not using them.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:27.870
Kevin also decides to bring a book with him
when he travels so he’ll have something

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:31.169
else to do with his downtime than doomscrolling
through Twitter.

00:05:31.169 --> 00:05:36.870
And that brings us to another major way your
cell phone is ripping you off: not by stealing

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.370
your money, but your time.

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:45.680
Almost every app and social platform on your
phone is explicitly designed to keep you engaged

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.740
well past the point of any benefit.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:52.500
Let’s be honest: just staring at the clouds
is probably a better use of your time than

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.550
arguing with your cousin on Facebook or fueling
your FOMO on Instagram.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:06:02.210
If switching from unlimited to pre-paid will
save you money and motivate you to cut down

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:05.960
on unproductive distractions, I’d call that
a win win.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.540
Over the last 20 years, cell phones have gone
from a luxury to a necessity.

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:15.210
But unlike most other utilities, the way we’re
charged for wireless service is inherently

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.210
vague.

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:20.670
As someone who likes to keep track of what
I’m getting for what I’m paying, I hate

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:21.670
that.

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:24.630
Fortunately, you don’t have to stumble around
in the dark.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.090
Check your data usage, look into alternate
companies and plans.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:32.900
It may take a bit of digging and a fair share
of discipline, but when it comes to your cell

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.760
phone, you--not your carrier--should be the
one making the calls.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.190
And that’s our two cents!

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:40.190
Data.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:42.010
It's data.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.010
Okay.

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:46.830
It's not the Star Trek character, it's data.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:48.440
Hey guys, it's us again!

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:53.030
So many of you leave such really wonderful,
thoughtful comments, questions, and we want

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:58.960
to take just a little bit of time at the end
of the video to address them.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.333
Miriam Korver, hello.

00:07:02.333 --> 00:07:06.590
She put that, "This video is applicable to
my situation pretty specifically.

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:12.320
I pay 850 in rent, which is over half my income."

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:13.320
Oof.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:14.360
That's tough.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:22.410
We generally recommend trying to keep your
housing at under 25% of your income, but $850

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:25.150
is not a high rent, that's an affordable rent.

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:30.970
To me, this kinda drives home the point of,
y'know, this viewer--and if anyone's in a

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:34.870
situation--doesn't necessarily just need to
do better themselves.

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:39.100
We as a society need to better for people
who are in situations like that.

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:45.910
There's a really great book about this whole
issue called "Evicted"--it actually won a

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:46.910
Pulitzer Prize.

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:54.100
His main thought is that we need to expand
these voucher programs for low-income families

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:59.350
that will cap how much of their income can
go to their basic needs.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:05.979
And he said something like 70% of the households
that are going through eviction have children.

00:08:05.979 --> 00:08:12.011
Eviction does happen in poverty but it can
also create poverty.

00:08:12.011 --> 00:08:15.360
I think this is a good one to leave on.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:20.430
Daniel Grooms put, "It's important to keep
asking "why?" until you actually reach the

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:22.710
bottom of the issue.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:25.490
Why can't landlords make money off of cheaper
housing?

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:30.139
Why won't communities allow low income housing
to be built?"

00:08:30.139 --> 00:08:31.820
Why communities won't allow this?

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:33.380
Y'know, a lot of us are scared.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:35.150
That something we need to work on.

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:40.070
That's something we shouldn't just be okay
with, that that's just the way things are.

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:42.950
Because that can change and it doesn't need
to be that way.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:48.850
So ask questions that you would like us to
look at and maybe even answer in our next

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:49.850
episode.

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:50.537
Yes, indeed.

00:08:50.537 --> 00:08:51.078
See you later.

